WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:00.720
Good morning, Hank. It’s Tuesday.

00:00:00.720 --> 00:00:08.240
So, when I think about advances in the field of medicine, I usually think about, you know, like, medicines: antibiotics, anesthesia, chemotherapy.

00:00:08.240 --> 00:00:15.040
And if I think harder, maybe I’ll consider
medical devices, like dialysis machines or artificial joints or refrigeration systems for vaccines,

00:00:15.040 --> 00:00:17.800
but I should be thinking about something else, too.

00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:27.320
So, this is Vibrio cholerae, the bacterium
that causes cholera, a diarrheal disease that usually only lasts for a few days, but can kill you very quickly via dehydration.

00:00:27.320 --> 00:00:29.680
It used to be one of the world’s biggest
health problems.

00:00:29.680 --> 00:00:38.480
About 160 years ago, a pandemic killed tens of millions of people, including over 150,000 Americans, one of whom was the U.S.’s 11th president, James K. Polk.

00:00:38.480 --> 00:00:42.800
Such was the fear of cholera that Polk was initially buried not with the fancy ceremony befitting a president,

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:47.120
but in a public cemetery as dictated by infectious disease regulations.

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:48.800
Cholera was terrifying.

00:00:48.810 --> 00:00:55.760
You could lose 20 pounds of body weight via diarrhea in under a day, and depending on the strain, it killed up to half of the people it infected.

00:00:55.760 --> 00:00:58.560
And today, for most of the world, it’s gone.

00:00:58.560 --> 00:01:01.500
Not because of antibiotics (although they
can shorten the course of the disease)

00:01:01.500 --> 00:01:05.700
or because of vaccines (although there is a vaccine that provides up to six months of protection),

00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:08.740
but because of sewers and water purification systems.

00:01:08.740 --> 00:01:13.580
Cholera is caused by Vibrio cholerae, but
it’s also caused by lack of access to clean water,

00:01:13.580 --> 00:01:20.540
which means that it’s not just a health problem; it’s also a social and political one, and we’re seeing that now in Yemen.

00:01:20.540 --> 00:01:25.200
So, around the world every year, more people have access to clean water and safe sanitation facilities,

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:35.120
but even before the Yemeni civil war that began in 2015, Yemen was one of the few countries on Earth where access to safe drinking water had actually declined since 1990.

00:01:35.120 --> 00:01:38.360
But since the war began, the situation has
become truly dire.

00:01:38.360 --> 00:01:41.960
The war has displaced millions of people and killed more than 10,000,

00:01:41.960 --> 00:01:49.420
and thousands more have died from what is the worst outbreak of cholera since at least 1949, when good records started being kept.

00:01:49.420 --> 00:01:56.820
The outbreak began after the bombing of Yemen’s capital, Sana’a, led to the failure of the city’s sewer system, and cholera has now spread through most of the country.

00:01:56.820 --> 00:02:02.420
The World Health Organization estimates that over 500,000 people have been infected, about half of them children.

00:02:02.420 --> 00:02:03.700
Now, cholera is treatable.

00:02:03.700 --> 00:02:07.180
The first line of treatment is oral rehydration solution, which is super cheap.

00:02:07.180 --> 00:02:11.440
In fact, versions can be made at home with just a few ingredients, but it requires clean water,

00:02:11.440 --> 00:02:13.690
which is in short supply in some parts of
Yemen.

00:02:13.690 --> 00:02:18.460
And the outbreak is complicated by the fact that most health workers in the hardest-hit regions haven’t been paid in over a year.

00:02:18.460 --> 00:02:23.640
Also, malnutrition is now common among Yemeni children, which lowers their resistance to infection.

00:02:23.640 --> 00:02:31.000
Despite these challenges, the cholera outbreak in Yemen has so far been less deadly than many previous outbreaks, partly due to the strains of cholera involved,

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:38.480
but also because nongovernmental organizations like Save the Children are working in extremely difficult conditions to provide oral rehydration solution.

00:02:38.480 --> 00:02:42.800
And many Yemeni health workers continue to provide care even as they go unpaid.

00:02:42.800 --> 00:02:48.600
But one of the hard truths about this epidemic is that fewer people would be dying if the world were paying more attention.

00:02:48.600 --> 00:02:51.540
I think there are a lot of reasons why we
find this story difficult to pay attention to.

00:02:51.540 --> 00:02:55.520
The civil war in Yemen is complex, and there’s no clear or simple path to peace.

00:02:55.520 --> 00:03:01.320
The political drama in the United States feels so astonishing and unprecedented that it’s difficult to look at anything else.

00:03:01.320 --> 00:03:08.340
And also, most of us don’t feel like we
can do anything about cholera in Yemen, but that is simply not true.

00:03:08.340 --> 00:03:14.420
By donating to organizations working in Yemen, we can help pay for health care workers and the hydration solution that saves so many lives,

00:03:14.420 --> 00:03:18.300
and I’d also argue that we make a difference just by paying attention.

00:03:18.300 --> 00:03:24.940
For one thing, it encourages more news coverage about the crisis, but it also helps shrink the huge empathy gap between the rich and poor worlds.

00:03:24.940 --> 00:03:30.140
When there’s a political or health crisis
in Europe or Australia, I pay more attention than I do to crises elsewhere,

00:03:30.140 --> 00:03:35.900
even though Europe and Australia are distant from me because the people who live in those countries feel close to me.

00:03:35.900 --> 00:03:38.840
I watch them on YouTube. I read their posts on Reddit.

00:03:38.840 --> 00:03:41.440
I really believe that all human lives are
equally valuable,

00:03:41.440 --> 00:03:48.400
but I can’t live that belief if I can’t empathize with the challenges faced by those who live in poverty or in fear of cholera.

00:03:48.400 --> 00:03:56.140
So I’m trying to pay attention because the
cholera outbreak in Yemen is a health problem and a political problem, and also an empathy problem.

00:03:56.140 --> 00:03:57.780
Hank, I’ll see you on Friday.

